're going to write nine  numbers in increasing order  now we will focus on the numbers only in  the first row  there are a total of nine numbers here  and now we can use the low shoot  technique to create a three by three  magic square from these 9 numbers  so let's go ahead and do that  we'll write the numbers in increasing  order starting from the column on the  right  then we will rotate these numbers 45  degrees counterclockwise to create a  diamond shape  we'll swap the numbers in the corners  and finally we'll compress this back  into a square  and this is a magic square  that involves the numbers from the first  row  the next step is to repeat the exact  same process  for the numbers in the second row  so we could write out these numbers in  ascending order starting from the column  on the right rotate them swap the corner  elements and put them back into a square  but there's a nice trick we can use  that's a shortcut  notice that all of the numbers in the  second row are exactly one more than the  numbers in the first row  so  74 is one more than 73 65 is one more  than 64 and so on  so how can we create a magic square from  the numbers in the second row  well the trick we can use is we can  simply add one to all of the numbers in  the magic square we've just created  because we're adding one to each element  this will still create a magic square  the sum will just be three more in each  row  so this will be a magic square that  involves all the numbers from the second  row  and you can see where this is going we  can actually create three by three magic  squares for every single row  for the numbers one through nine  so i'm not going to do that right now  that'll be too many numbers to put on  the screen  but just keep in mind we can do that and  we're go that's how you can use the low  shoe method to create three by three  magic squares of every single one of  these nine rows  once you create these three by three  squares the question is how are you  going to position them to create a nine  by nine magic square  so the next step is to position these  three by three squares  so notice in the original magic square  we have the numbers one through nine and  there are nine rows that we want to put  three by three grids  so we look where the one is  this magic square that we've created has  a one  and the one in the original magic square  is in  the last row in the second column  so we're going to delete that one and  put this entire magic square this three  by three magic square in that position  we're going to repeat this for the  number two  so the number two is in the upper right  corner of the original magic square  so we're going to delete that element  and now we need to put in the three by  three magic square that involves the  number two  we obtain that by adding one to every  single number of the three by three  magic square that involves one  we then repeat the process for the  number three  the number three is in the second row in  the first column  we delete it and now we create a three  by three magic square involving the  number three which we obtained by adding  one to each of the elements of the three  by three magic square involving the  number two  so this is kind of a recursive method to  create a nine by nine magic square  and now we repeat this process for the  numbers four five six seven eight and  nine  so i'll go ahead and skip all these  steps and this is the result you get  this is a nine by nine magic square  which is built up from small three by  three magic squares  that we started out using the low shoe  technique  and  each of these three by three magic  squares is a magic square itself  and i put in bold where the numbers are  so we have four nine two three five  seven  eight one and six and those come from  the original magic square which was on  the our magical turtle  now the amazing thing is that this nine  by nine arrangement of numbers  every single row column and diagonal  adds up 